examples.question_text:
(a) A rectangle has area $39.6\text{ m}^2$ and length $6\text{ m}$. Find its width.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (6,3);
\node[below] at (3,0) {$6\text{ m}$};
\node[right] at (6,1.5) {$w$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b) A parallelogram has area $84\text{ cm}^2$ and base $12\text{ cm}$. Find its perpendicular height.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(6,0)--(7.5,3.5)--(1.5,3.5)--cycle;
\node[below] at (3,0) {$12\text{ cm}$};
\draw[dashed] (2,0)--(2,3.5);
\node[right] at (2,1.75) {$h$};
\draw (2,0.3)--(2.3,0.3)--(2.3,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A triangle has area $55\text{ cm}^2$ and perpendicular height $10\text{ cm}$ to the base. A slanted side is $13\text{ cm}$. Find the base length.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (11,0);
\coordinate (C) at ({sqrt(69)},10);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\node[below] at (5.5,0) {$b$};
\path (A)--(C) node[midway,above left] {$13\text{ cm}$};
\draw[dashed] ({sqrt(69)},0)--(C);
\node[left] at ({sqrt(69)},4) {$10\text{ cm}$};
\draw ({sqrt(69)},0.5)--({sqrt(69)+0.5},0.5)--({sqrt(69)+0.5},0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) A parallelogram has a base of $15\text{ cm}$ and perpendicular height $8\text{ cm}$. Another side is $10\text{ cm}$. Find the perpendicular height to that side.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=3mm,y=3mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\usetikzlibrary{calc,angles}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (15,0);
\coordinate (C) at (21,8);
\coordinate (D) at (6,8);
\coordinate (F) at ($(B)!(D)!(C)$);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\node[below] at (7.5,0) {$15\text{ cm}$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,below right] {$10\text{ cm}$};
\draw[dashed] (7,0)--(7,8);
\node[left] at (7,3) {$8\text{ cm}$};
\draw (7,0.7)--(7.7,0.7)--(7.7,0);
\draw[dashed] (D)--(F);
\path (D)--(F) node[midway,above] {$h$};
\pic[draw,angle radius=2mm] {right angle=C--F--D};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b) A triangle has base $21\text{ cm}$ and perpendicular height $8\text{ cm}$. A second perpendicular height of $12\text{ cm}$ is drawn to another side. Find the length of that side.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=3mm,y=3mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\usetikzlibrary{calc,angles}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (21,0);
\coordinate (C) at ({21-sqrt(132)},8);
\coordinate (H) at ({21-sqrt(132)},0);
\coordinate (F) at ($(B)!(A)!(C)$);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\node[below] at (10.5,0) {$21\text{ cm}$};
\path (B)--(C) node[midway,above right] {$b$};
\draw[dashed] (H)--(C);
\path (H)--(C) node[pos=0.3,right] {$8\text{ cm}$};
\draw ({21-sqrt(132)},0.7)--({21-sqrt(132)+0.7},0.7)--({21-sqrt(132)+0.7},0);
\draw[dashed] (C)--(F);
\draw[dashed] (A)--(F);
\path (A)--(F) node[pos=0.45,above left] {$12\text{ cm}$};
\pic[draw,angle radius=2mm] {right angle=C--F--A};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A rectangle is $10\text{ cm}$ by $6\text{ cm}$. A triangle has half its area and base $12\text{ cm}$. Find the triangle’s perpendicular height.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (10,6);
\node[below] at (5,0) {$10\text{ cm}$};
\node[left] at (0,3) {$6\text{ cm}$};
\draw (16,0)--(28,0)--(21,5)--cycle;
\node[below] at (22,0) {$12\text{ cm}$};
\draw[dashed] (21,0)--(21,5);
\node[right] at (21,2.5) {$h$};
\draw (21,0.5)--(21.5,0.5)--(21.5,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A smaller rectangle of area $21\text{ cm}^2$ and height $3\text{ cm}$ sits on the left of a larger rectangle. The larger rectangle extends $8\text{ cm}$ beyond it to the right and has area $150\text{ cm}^2$. Find the larger rectangle’s height.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\draw (0,10) rectangle (7,13);
\node at (7.5,5) {$150\text{ cm}^2$};
\node at (3.5,11.5) {$21\text{ cm}^2$};
\node[left] at (0,11.5) {$3\text{ cm}$};
\node[right] at (15,5) {$h$};
\draw[thin] (7,13)--(7,15);
\draw[thin] (15,10)--(15,15);
\draw[<->] (7,14.5)--(15,14.5);
\node[above] at (11,14.5) {$8\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A parallelogram is divided into two smaller parallelograms with the same perpendicular height. The right part has base $15\text{ cm}$ and area $75\text{ cm}^2$. The left part has area $45\text{ cm}^2$. Find its base length.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=3mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(24,0)--(27,5)--(3,5)--cycle;
\draw (9,0)--(12,5);
\node at (6,2.5) {$45\text{ cm}^2$};
\node at (18,2.5) {$75\text{ cm}^2$};
\node[below] at (4.5,0) {$b$};
\node[below] at (16.5,0) {$15\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
